-- ===========================================================================
-- 0005_recovery_list_indexes — the two indexes the recovery list runs on.
--
-- Generated by drizzle-kit from packages/db/src/schema/shop.ts (the two new
-- `index(...).where(...)` declarations), then edited by hand in exactly two
-- ways, both recorded here because 0002 set the precedent that a hand-edit to a
-- generated file is stated rather than left to be noticed:
--
--   1. the two statements were SWAPPED, so the file reads in the order spec 003
--      technical-considerations §10 numbers them (step 8 then step 9) and so the
--      196x statement is the first thing read. Neither index depends on the
--      other and neither references the other's table, so this ordering is
--      presentational and nothing else — see ORDERING below for the one
--      ordering rule in this file that IS load-bearing; and
--   2. the annotations. This file, not the TypeScript, is what a reviewer who
--      does not know Drizzle reads.
--
-- Nothing else changed: `CREATE INDEX ... USING btree (...) WHERE ...` is
-- exactly what drizzle-kit emitted for both.
--
-- WHAT THIS BUYS
--
-- Spec 003 §2.4 — *"a person can find every purchase that was paid for but
-- never delivered"*. That list is a QUERY, not a queue: no claim, no fan-out,
-- no ordering to maintain, nothing to go stale (technical-considerations §3,
-- "Rejected"). The price of that simplification is that the query reads the
-- whole shop every time it is asked, so it has to be cheap. These two indexes
-- are what make it cheap. Nothing consumes them yet — `UndeliveredOrdersService`
-- and `GET /api/admin/orders/undelivered` are the next task in this slice — and
-- they ship first so the endpoint is never the thing that introduces a 1.7 s
-- sequential scan.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- MEASURED — Postgres 16.11, this project's container, EXPLAIN (ANALYZE,
-- BUFFERS) inside a rolled-back transaction against a fixture of 20 000 orders
-- / 18 401 deliveries / 21 200 attempts / 22 050 payment events, leaving 1 599
-- paid-but-undelivered (technical-considerations §5). Not estimates.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
--   indexes present                    execution     buffers   dominant cost
--   -------------------------------- ------------ ---------- ----------------------------
--   Phase 1 indexes only               1 723.8 ms    443 383   Seq Scan on payment_events
--                                                              x 1 599 = 436 527 buffers
--   + payment_events_paid_order_idx       12.7 ms     10 627   --
--     and 0002's UNIQUE (order_id,
--     attempt)
--   + orders_undelivered_idx               8.7 ms     11 465   --
--
--   Build cost: payment_events_paid_order_idx 10.7 ms, SHARE lock
--               orders_undelivered_idx         6.0 ms, SHARE lock
--
-- 196x, and the finding worth carrying out of it is not the number. **The
-- expensive part was never the per-order-latest-row problem everyone looks at.**
-- Three different strategies for "the newest issuance attempt per order" —
-- LEFT JOIN LATERAL, DISTINCT ON, five correlated subqueries — were measured,
-- and the first two came out within 5 % of each other, because all of them were
-- carrying the same `payment_events` sequential scan underneath. It was
-- `paid_at`. It is invisible if you read the plan for row counts and only
-- shows up in `Buffers`.
--
-- Note the second row honestly: buffers go UP (10 627 -> 11 465) when
-- `orders_undelivered_idx` is added, because the plan now reads that index's
-- own pages. It buys 4 ms, 480 kB of index that is not scanned, and residency —
-- not fewer buffers. See "HONEST ACCOUNTING" under (2).
--
-- RE-VERIFIED ON THIS MACHINE when these indexes were written, because a quoted
-- measurement nobody re-ran is a claim rather than evidence. Same Postgres
-- 16.11 container, same fixture shape (20 000 orders / 18 401 deliveries /
-- 21 200 attempts / 22 050 payment events / 1 599 paid-but-undelivered), §4's
-- query verbatim, the whole of it inside a rolled-back transaction:
--
--   without these two indexes ... 3 832.7 ms  SubPlan 1 -> Seq Scan on
--                                             payment_events, 1 599 loops,
--                                             1 255 215 buffers on that node
--   with them ...................   11.4 ms  Index Scan using
--                                             orders_undelivered_idx, plus
--                                             InitPlan 1 -> Limit -> Index Only
--                                             Scan using
--                                             payment_events_paid_order_idx
--
-- The absolute numbers differ from §5's above — different host, different cache
-- — and the SHAPE is what was being checked: the same dominant cost, the same
-- two plan nodes, the same order of magnitude. Two details from that run are
-- worth recording, because a reader could mistake either one for a fault:
--
--   * `Index Scan using orders_undelivered_idx` carries NO `Index Cond` and no
--     `Filter`. That absence IS the proof the partial index is doing its job:
--     the planner proved the predicate implies the WHERE clause and dropped the
--     status test altogether. A `Filter: (status = ANY ...)` on that node would
--     mean exactly the opposite — see the literals-versus-`= ANY($1)` note
--     under (2).
--   * A fixture whose undelivered orders sit CONTIGUOUSLY at the end of the
--     heap makes the planner choose `orders_status_idx` instead, because the
--     heap access behind any index then looks sequential and the two indexes
--     cost the same. The run above scatters them, as a real shop's are. Worth
--     knowing before concluding from a fixture that this index goes unused.
--
-- Index size, same run: 136 kB for `orders_undelivered_idx` against 288 kB for
-- `orders_status_idx`. §5 records 72 kB against 552 kB. The two readings were
-- taken differently — §5 built both indexes AFTER loading the fixture, and
-- `CREATE INDEX` packs to a 90 % fillfactor and deduplicates, where rows
-- inserted into an index that already exists leave pages around half full — so
-- the bytes do not transfer between runs. What does transfer, and what this
-- index is justified on, is the direction: on the same rows the partial index
-- is a fraction of the full-column one, because it holds only the orders that
-- are still unfinished business.
--
-- `Heap Fetches: 1599`, not 0, on the measured plan: the fixture was inserted
-- seconds earlier and never vacuumed, so the visibility map was empty. On a
-- table autovacuum has visited this becomes a true index-only scan. Stated
-- rather than quoting a `Heap Fetches: 0` that was not measured.
--
-- NOTHING HERE REWRITES A TABLE — `CREATE INDEX` never does, and it is checked
-- rather than asserted: `pg_class.relfilenode` for `orders` and for
-- `payment_events` is unchanged across this migration. Each statement takes a
-- `SHARE` lock on its table, which blocks writes to that table for the duration
-- (10.7 ms and 6.0 ms) and blocks no reads at all.
--
-- `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` IS NOT AVAILABLE TO THIS PROJECT, and this is the
-- file where that matters most, so it is stated here as well as in 0002.
-- `packages/db/src/migrate.ts` calls Drizzle's migrator, whose pg dialect opens
-- ONE transaction spanning every pending file and every
-- `drizzle.__drizzle_migrations` insert; `CONCURRENTLY` cannot run inside a
-- transaction block. Using it would mean taking these two statements out of the
-- migration runner entirely — a second, manual deployment step, and an index
-- that can be left `INVALID` by a failed build with nothing rolling it back. At
-- 10.7 ms and 6.0 ms against the largest fixture this shop will ever see, the
-- trade is not worth making. Stating the constraint now is cheaper than
-- discovering it during an incident.
--
-- ORDERING — the rule in this file that is not free to move.
--
--   * `orders_undelivered_idx` NAMES 'delivery_failed' IN ITS PREDICATE, so it
--     cannot be created before `orders_status_check` admits that value. That
--     widening is 0001_delivery_failed_status, which has shipped, and the
--     migrator applies files in journal order — so the dependency is satisfied
--     by the file numbering and by nothing else. Do not renumber this file
--     below 0001, and do not "simplify" 0001 away.
--   * Within this file, nothing. The two statements touch different tables and
--     neither reads the other. They were swapped for readability (see the
--     header) and swapping them back would be harmless.
--
-- Scope: this file is the two indexes and nothing else. The query that uses
-- them (technical-considerations §4) and the endpoint that runs it are the next
-- task in this slice.
-- ===========================================================================

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- (1) THE SINGLE LARGEST WIN IN THIS PHASE: 1 723.8 ms -> 12.7 ms.
--
-- The recovery list has to answer "when was this paid for" per listed order,
-- and the shop has no `orders.paid_at` column — deliberately, because writing
-- one would need a `CASE` inside the single generic status-guarded UPDATE that
-- every transition shares, and that statement's whole value is that it is data
-- rather than a special case per transition (technical-considerations §3,
-- "Rejected"). The fact is derived from the payment inbox instead:
--
--   SELECT min(pe.received_at) FROM payment_events pe
--    WHERE pe.order_id = o.id AND pe.status = 'paid';
--   -- NULL => paid with no `paid` event on file. The order is LISTED, not
--   --         hidden: an order in a paid-onward status with no paid event is
--   --         precisely the sort of thing an operator must be shown.
--   -- Plan with this index: InitPlan -> Limit -> Index Only Scan using
--   --         payment_events_paid_order_idx.
--   -- Plan without it:      Seq Scan on payment_events, ONCE PER LISTED ORDER.
--   --         1 599 of them on the fixture: 436 527 buffers, and effectively
--   --         the entire 1 723.8 ms.
--
-- BOTH COLUMNS EARN THEIR PLACE. `order_id` leads because that is the
-- correlation; `received_at` follows because the planner rewrites `min()` over
-- an indexed column into "read the first row of an ordered scan and stop"
-- (InitPlan -> Limit). Without the second column that becomes an aggregate over
-- every `paid` event for the order. It is also a covering pair: the scan never
-- touches the heap for a value, only for visibility.
--
-- WHY PARTIAL, AND WHY THIS PREDICATE IS THE STRONGEST ONE IN THE SCHEMA.
-- `payment_events_unprocessed_order_idx` (0000_init) is partial on
-- `processed_at IS NULL` — a predicate rows LEAVE, which is sound and is what
-- keeps that index to a handful of entries. This one is partial on
-- `status = 'paid'`, a predicate a row can never leave in either direction:
-- `payment_events.status` is written once by the INSERT that records the event
-- and is never UPDATEd anywhere in the codebase. Membership is fixed at birth.
-- The caveat attached to (2) below — a predicate over a value whose meaning a
-- later phase can widen — cannot arise here.
--
-- The column has no CHECK constraint and deliberately so (0000_init): it holds
-- a value from a system we do not control, and persist-before-process means an
-- unrecognised status must still be stored. That does not weaken this index —
-- an unrecognised status is simply not `paid`, so the row is not in it, which
-- is correct. `status = 'paid'` is the shop's own reading of the provider's
-- word, not a constraint on what the provider may say.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE INDEX "payment_events_paid_order_idx" ON "payment_events" USING btree ("order_id","received_at") WHERE "status" = 'paid';--> statement-breakpoint

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- (2) THE DRIVING SCAN — "paid, and holding no key".
--
--   SELECT ... FROM orders o
--    WHERE o.status IN ('paid', 'delivering', 'out_of_stock', 'delivery_failed')
--      AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM deliveries d WHERE d.order_id = o.id)
--    ORDER BY paid_at ASC NULLS LAST, o.created_at ASC
--    LIMIT 200;
--   -- 0 rows => THERE IS NOTHING TO RECOVER. Not an error, and not a blank
--   --           screen: §2.4 requires the operator be told so in words. It is
--   --           the only zero-row case in this project that is a complete
--   --           answer rather than a signal that somebody else got there first.
--
-- The four statuses are "paid, and holding no key" — the in-flight set minus
-- `created` (nobody has paid), plus the recoverable pair. It is WIDER THAN
-- "STUCK" on purpose (§4, A5): `paid` and `delivering` are in the list so an
-- order whose worker died between the claim and the outcome write is visible,
-- which is the one class of stuck order that nothing automatic can reach and
-- therefore the one class that is otherwise invisible. Whether such an order
-- may be RETRIED is a separate and narrower question, answered by a
-- status-guarded UPDATE and never by this list.
--
-- THE STATUS LIST MUST BE WRITTEN AS LITERALS IN THE QUERY, NOT `= ANY($1)`.
-- This is the one place the codebase's own bound-parameter convention must not
-- be copied. Measured, both plans against this index present:
--
--     literals:            Index Scan using orders_undelivered_idx
--     = ANY($1), generic:  Seq Scan on orders   Filter: (status = ANY ($1))
--
-- Postgres cannot prove that a value it has not seen implies a partial index's
-- predicate. Custom planning saves it today — `client.ts` forbids `.prepare()`,
-- so every statement is planned against its actual values — but that is a
-- planner heuristic protecting the query, not a property of the query. The
-- `= ANY($3)` convention elsewhere in this codebase exists so that a
-- transition's statement text stays stable across `from`-lists of different
-- lengths, and there is no partial index there for a parameter to hide a
-- predicate from.
--
-- WHY A PARTIAL INDEX IS SOUND HERE. Same argument as
-- `payment_events_unprocessed_order_idx`: the predicate is stable in the
-- direction that matters, because rows leave the set and never come back. An
-- order can move INSIDE the set (`out_of_stock -> delivering` on a retry), but
-- the two ways out — `delivered` and `payment_failed` — are terminal by I9, and
-- I9 is enforced by every transition naming its permitted source states. So the
-- index holds exactly the shop's unfinished business and shrinks as that
-- business is finished: 72 kB against `orders_status_idx`'s 552 kB on the same
-- 20 000 rows.
--
-- HONEST ACCOUNTING. This index saves 4 ms on a 12.7 ms query and its buffer
-- count goes UP. It is the smallest of the phase's index changes by a wide
-- margin, and it earns its place on size and residency — 72 kB stays in cache
-- next to a table this query otherwise scans in full — not on the milliseconds.
-- If it were the only change in this file it would be hard to justify. It is
-- not; (1) is.
--
-- ###########################################################################
-- # CAVEAT — READ THIS BEFORE ADDING A STATUS.                              #
-- #                                                                         #
-- # A PARTIAL INDEX WHOSE PREDICATE IS A STATUS LIST MUST BE REBUILT IF A    #
-- # LATER PHASE ADDS ANOTHER UNDELIVERED STATUS, AND THE FAILURE MODE IS     #
-- # NOT AN ERROR — IT IS THE NEW STATUS SILENTLY FALLING OUT OF THE          #
-- # OPERATOR'S LIST.                                                        #
-- ###########################################################################
--
-- Nothing raises. Nothing logs. The query still runs, this index is still
-- chosen, and orders in the new status are simply not in it, so they are not
-- returned — and the one screen whose entire purpose is "nothing that was paid
-- for is invisible" quietly stops being true, on exactly the orders somebody
-- added a new status for because they were unusual. It is the same shape of
-- failure as a `WHERE` clause that silently matches nothing, one level further
-- down where no test looks.
--
-- What to do instead: widening `undeliveredOrderStatuses` in
-- `packages/db/src/schema/shop.ts` is a MIGRATION. drizzle-kit will emit the
-- `DROP INDEX` / `CREATE INDEX` pair when that array changes, because the
-- predicate is generated from it — that is the one mechanical protection there
-- is, and it only fires if the array is the thing that changes. A status added
-- to `packages/contracts`' `recoverableOrderStatuses` and NOT to that array
-- produces no diff, no migration, and no complaint from anything.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE INDEX "orders_undelivered_idx" ON "orders" USING btree ("created_at") WHERE "status" IN ('paid', 'delivering', 'out_of_stock', 'delivery_failed');
